<p>These are, perhaps, not the very best times for humanities majors. Seldom do we even hear the once-standard defense that, by pursuing a course of study in subjects of that kind, students “learn how to think.” Such a justification still points to real value, at least in the most demanding settings. Take Harvard’s course Humanities 10, whose digital device-free environment and syllabus covering everything from <em>The Odyssey</em> and&nbsp;<em>Ulysses</em> to&nbsp;Plato and James Baldwin — at a pace of one substantial book per week — recently <a href="https://www.wsj.com/lifestyle/harvard-class-humanities-32e06366?st=Gtw1e6&amp;reflink=desktopwebshare_permalink">drew the attention of the&nbsp;<em>Wall Street Journal</em></a>. Yet it’s always been true that you don’t have to major in anything to learn how to think, or in any case, to make a start of doing so.</p>
<p>Take the philosophy-and-literature YouTuber Jared Henderson, who in <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tiZ5tb8Idlg">the video at the top of the post</a> tells us how he began his self-directed study in high school. “I did what a lot of people would think is natural,” he says. “I went to a bookstore, and then I just found some books that I wanted to read.” The pieces of light reading he took home: Friedrich Nietzsche’s <em>The Birth of Tragedy</em> and Martin Heidegger’s&nbsp;<em>Introduction to</em> <em>Metaphysics</em>.</p>
